      "text": "To find the ratio of \\( AC \\) to \\( BD \\), we need to determine the lengths of \\( AC \\) and \\( BD \\) first.\n\n1. **Identify the positions of the points and their distances:**\n   Points \\( A \\), \\( B \\), \\( C \\), and \\( D \\) lie on a line in that order. The given distances are:\n   - \\( AB = 2 \\) units\n   - \\( BC = 5 \\) units\n   - \\( AD = 14 \\) units\n\n2. **Determine the length \\( CD \\):**\n   The total length of segment \\( AD \\) is the sum of \\( AB \\), \\( BC \\), and \\( CD \\):\n   \\[\n   AD = AB + BC + CD\n   \\]\n   Substituting the known values:\n   \\[\n   14 = 2 + 5 + CD\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   14 = 7 + CD\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   CD = 14 - 7\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   CD = 7 \\text{ units}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Calculate the length \\( AC \\):**\n   \\( AC \\) is the sum of \\( AB \\) and \\( BC \\):\n   \\[\n   AC = AB + BC\n   \\]\n   Substituting the known values:\n   \\[\n   AC = 2 + 5 = 7 \\text{ units}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Calculate the length \\( BD \\):**\n   \\( BD \\) is the sum of \\( AB \\), \\( BC \\), and \\( CD \\):\n   \\[\n   BD = AB + BC + CD\n   \\]\n   Substituting the known values:\n   \\[\n   BD = 2 + 5 + 7 = 14 \\text{ units}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Determine the ratio \\( AC:BD \\):**\n   Finally, we find the ratio of \\( AC \\) to \\( BD \\):\n   \\[\n   \\text{Ratio} = \\frac{AC}{BD} = \\frac{7}{14}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Simplify the fraction:**\n   The fraction \\(\\frac{7}{14}\\) can be simplified by dividing both the numerator and the denominator by their greatest common divisor, which is 7:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{7}{14} = \\frac{7 \\div 7}{14 \\div 7} = \\frac{1}{2}\n   \\]\n\n### Conclusion: \nThe ratio of \\( AC \\) to \\( BD \\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{2}}\n\\]",
